/bin/cc from the Micronix 1.6 distribution

There is no source for the distribution's cc.  cc.c in this directory
is the tree's own v7-cc port, swizzled onto the Whitesmiths toolchain;
it is a different program and builds a different binary, and it is the
one installed as /bin/cc.  This directory also carries the
distribution binary and its disassembly, following the method used for
cmd/td, cmd/newuser and cmd/print.

The vendor preprocessor has no source either.  It is here as wpp.dist
and installs as /bin/wpp, because cpp is now the tree's own
preprocessor (cmd/cpp, the Reiser preprocessor).  cc.c names the pass
wpp in its table for the same reason.

cc.dist is the binary off inode 29 of disks/dist/1014-8_c_1.IMD, 13706
bytes, mode 0711, root, 7 August 1982.  It is identical to the one in
filesystem/bin.

The binary
----------
It is a Whitesmiths object/executable: 99 94, no symbols, no relocs,
text at 0100 for 323d bytes, data at 4000 for 33d bytes, no bss and no
heap.  It is the Whitesmiths C compiler supervisor: it drives the
native pipeline (cpp, cp1, cp2, as, link) and the CP/M pipeline (pp,
p1, p2, p3, an, ptc).  The usage text in the binary is

	usage: pp -[c d*^ i* o* p? s? x 6] <files>

and the program does not consult argv[0] for it.  Its string table
contains both "cpp" and "pp", but this vendor binary is not what runs:
the installed cc is cc.c, which invokes wpp.

The disassembly
---------------
	src/tools/disas -f cc.ctl cc.dist > cc.dis

disas needs the data-range fix in src/tools/disas.c: a byte range
marked with -d or `bytes` now overrides a CODE target.  Without it,
the code tracer follows a non-returning call into the string constants
and disassembles "/include/" as instructions.

cc.ctl is the control file.  It marks every string run embedded in the
text as `bytes`; without those marks the text/string mix in this
binary makes the listing useless.  It also names the two runtime pairs
and the program's functions.

What the listing shows
----------------------
	0100	jump over the startup
	010b	crt startup; calls main at 014a, then exit at 223f
	014a	main: saves argc at 414a and argv at 4148, calls setup
		and opts, then 1ae1(1) and c.ret
	01ec	setup: starts by walking signals 1..15: signal(sig,1) to
		ignore, and 1ae1 installed where the old handler was not
		ignore; continues with the option/setup work; ends 0595
	05a7	opts: walks argc/argv (DE+4/DE+6), classifies arguments
		against the suffix strings at 0598 (.s .8 .c .p .c),
		calls 1a16 (suffix/string test), 2482 and 2bba (string
		helpers), and stores the results in the data globals;
		early return at 0626, end 08d5
	0af3	runcpm: sh -c, the pp | p1 | p2 pipeline
	0c00	runcpmx: sh -c, the pp | p1 | p2 | an pipeline
	08df	runptc: builds the ptc command from "ptc" and "-o"; ends 0972
	098d	runcpp: cpp/pp runner
	0ce5	runcp1: cp1/p1 runner
	0e65	runcp2: cp2/p2 runner
	0fad	runp3: p3 runner
	1073	runas: as/an runner
	1274	runlink: link/ld runner
	17fd	run: the common runner.  If the verbose flag (400c) is set,
		print each argv word; ignore the signals; fork, exec and
		wait.  The runners above build their argv from the string
		block immediately before each one.
	1ae1	the signal handler installed by setup; called with 1
	32e2	c.ent, the first runtime prologue
	32e9	c.ret, its epilogue
	32ed	c.ent2, the register-variable prologue
	3303	c.ret2, its epilogue
	4337	r2, 4339 r3, 433b r4, the register-variable cells
	4000	data: the program's globals, including 4148 (argv) and
		414a (argc) from the direct stores in main

The suffix pointers pushed by opts are 05a1 (".p") and 05a4 (".c"),
not functions: opts compares an argument against those strings.

The runners are the vendor binary's split of the same idea as run() in
the tree's cc.c: each tool has its own small function that lays out the
argument vector (the strings just before it), and run at 17fd does the
common verbose print, signal handling, fork/exec/wait.  runcpm and
runcpmx instead build a "sh -c" pipeline for the CP/M tools.

The program's own code is interleaved with its string literals from
0100 to about 1e00; the library follows, and the errno message table is
at 2767.  cc.dis is the complete listing.  Reading it back to C, the
way td.c and newuser.c were written, is the next step; cc.c is not
that read-back.
